verbatim, 671)\n15. num:dist_evals_per_catchment → need to check the Wiley page (403). Try alternatives; else UNREACHABLE.\n\nLet me try to fetch the Laloy & Vrugt page via alternative means. Let me search for the quote.\n\nActually, let me also reconsider card:left_open. The text: \"No published result times batched GPU MCMC giving all 671 CAMELS catchments full Bayesian GR4J treatment, so the crossover is unmeasured [s_mgb].\" \n\nThe citation [s_mgb] is attached to this whole statement. The source s_mgb is the Freitas et al. MGB paper. Does it support \"No published result times batched GPU MCMC on CAMELS\"? No — a single paper can't support a universal negative. But it does support that the closest prior (MGB) is a different thing (not batched MCMC, not GR4J, not CAMELS). So the \"so the crossover is unmeasured\" is a reasonable inference IF we accept \"no published result exists\". But the source doesn't establish the negative.\n\nI'll mark UNSUPPORTED for left_open, noting that s_mgb only describes one related paper and cannot establish that no published result exists; the negative claim is unverified.\n\nHmm, actually, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe \"left_open\" should be evaluated as: the factual claim is \"No published result times batched GPU MCMC giving all 671 CAMELS catchments full Bayesian GR4J treatment\". This is a claim about the literature. The cited source s_mgb is the closest prior (MGB GPU optimization), which is used to illustrate what HAS been done (so we can infer what hasn't). But strictly, the source doesn't support the negative claim. UNSUPPORTED is correct.\n\nBut actually, is it CONTRADICTED? No, the source doesn't contradict it; it just doesn't support it. So UNSUPPORTED (not CONTRADICTED).\n\nNow let me also double check card:closest_prior more carefully. Text: \"Freitas et al. 2022 GPU-optimized a hydrological model's calibration to 20x CPU speedup, on one distributed model, not batched MCMC across many GR4J catchments [s_mgb].\"\n\nThe source: title \"Performance optimization of the MGB hydrological model for multi-core and GPU architectures\", abstract \"This work aims at performance optimizations on the MGB... hydrological model and the MOCOM-UA calibration method for two watersheds... Significant speedups of up to 20 × (CPU) were achieved for calibration... 24 × (CPU) and 65 × (GPU) for simulations...\".\n\nClaims:\n- \"Freitas et al. 2022\" ✓ (Freitas, Mendes, Ilic 2022)\n- \"GPU-optimized a hydrological model's calibration\" — the paper optimizes for multi-core AND GPU. The calibration speedup is 20x (CPU). The \"GPU-optimized\" is partially accurate (it targets GPU too), but the 20x is CPU. The card says \"GPU-optimized... to 20x CPU speedup\" — so it correctly labels the 20x as CPU. ✓\n- \"to 20x CPU speedup\" ✓ (\"up to 20× (CPU) ... for calibration\")\n- \"on one distributed model\" — MGB is a distributed model (large-basin). The page describes \"the MGB hydrological model\" (one model). Actually, is MGB explicitly \"distributed\"? The abstract says \"Large-scale hydrological models simulate watershed processes\". MGB = \"Modelo de Grandes Bacias\" (Model of Large Basins), a distributed hydrological model. The page title/abstract refers to \"the MGB hydrological model\" (one model). \"on one distributed model\" is a reasonable characterization. The page says \"for two watersheds\" (two catchments). \"one distributed model\" — the model is one (MGB), applied to two watersheds. ✓\n- \"not batched MCMC across many GR4J catchments\" — the paper uses MOCOM-UA calibration (not MCMC), on MGB (not GR4J), on two watersheds (not many catchments). ✓ The page confirms MOCOM-UA (Multi-Objective Complex Evolution), not MCMC.\n\nSo closest_prior is SUPPORTED. The quote appears verbatim in the abstract. Good.\n\nActually, let me note the quote in s_mgb is the abstract, which I fetched and it appears verbatim. Good.\n\nNow, one more consideration: For card:closest_prior and card:left_open, they cite source id \"s_mgb\". The s_mgb source item itself is in the list. The quote for s_mgb matches. So the citation is valid and the quote is real.\n\nNow, the num:dist_evals_per_catchment. Let me try to find the quote. The quote: \"Previous results presented by Keating et al. [2010] have shown that DREAM required about 50 million surrogate model evaluations to converge to a limiting parameter distribution\". This is from Laloy & Vrugt 2012 (WRR, \"High-dimensional posterior exploration of hydrologic models using multiple-try DREAM(ZS) and high-performance computing\"). DOI 10.1029/2011WR010608.\n\nThe Wiley page returned 403. Let me try:\n1. Search for the quote.\n2. Try a different URL (e.g., the PDF, or a preprint).\n\nLet me search.\n\nAlso I should double check whether the page might be reachable via webfetch with a different URL format. Wiley's onlinelibrary often returns 403 to bots. Let me try the search approach to confirm the quote text exists somewhere reachable.\n\nLet me do a websearch for the quote."
