    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_7f14f531a443c0f4a0f1501077f6d833\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://rdrr.io/rforge/dream/man/dream.html\",\n      \"title\": \"dream: DiffeRential Evolution Adaptive Metropolis (DREAM) in dream: DiffeRential Evolution Adaptive Metropolis\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2016-03-16\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Arguments\\n|`FUN` |model function with first argument a vector of parameter values of\\nlength ndim. |\\n| --- | --- |\\n|`func.type` |type of value `FUN` returns. One of:\\nDetails\\n| _Element_ | _Default_ | _Description_ |\\n| values outside of parameter bounds. |  |  |\\n| One of: \\\"reflect\\\", \\\"bound\\\", \\\"fold\\\", \\\"none\\\",\\\"rand\\\" |  |  |\\n| `burnin.length` | 0.1 | If `<1` proportion of |\\n| function evaluations to use as burn-in period. If `>1` number |  |  |\\n| of function evaluations to use. |  |  |\\nDetails\\n| _Element_ | _Default_ | _Description_ |\\n| `ndraw` | 1e5 | Maximum number of function evaluations. May |\\n| terminate before convergence. |  |  |\\n| `maxtime` | Inf | Maximum duration of optimization in seconds. May |\\n| terminate before convergence. |  |  |\\n| `Rthres` | 1.01 | Value of Gelman & Rubin's convergence |\\nDetails\\nDefault settings may not be sufficient for model to run. For few\\nparameters, nseq may need to be increased.\\nIf dream frequently warns that it is reentering the burn-period, `control$burnin.length` may need to be increased.\\nValue\\n| `X` | converged nseq points in parameter space. matrix `nseq x ndim` . |\\n| chains to the posterior distribution. (N.B. in nonlinear |  |\\n| optimisation, this is not a measure of convergence to an optimum parameter set) |  |\\n| `CR` | Probability of crossover at each step. matrix `n.elem/steps x |\\n| 1+length(pCR)` . |  |\\nValue\\n| `X` | converged nseq points in parameter space. matrix `nseq x ndim` . |\\n| `in.burnin` | Boolean showing whether dream was in the burn-in |\\n| period at termination (see description above). |  |\\n| `fun.evals` | Total number of function evaluations. |\\n| `time` | running time (wall time) in seconds. |\\nSee Also\\n* `demo(example1)` Fitting a banana shaped distribution - as in the original matlab code\\n* `demo(example2)` Fitting an n-dimensional Gaussian distribution as\\n  in the original matlab code\\n[dream documentation](/rforge/dream/) built on May 2, 2019, 5:21 p.m.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://iris.unitn.it/retrieve/17056254-cdee-40f7-85ea-c2a3c495f181/1-s2.0-S0951832025002601-main%20%281%29.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"Surrogate modeling for probability distribution estimation: Uniform or adaptive design?\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2025-05-08\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"loss functions, we identify an optimal function that closely resembles the original simulator. The properties of  rely on the selected types of surrogate models and their configurations and parameters.\\nThe configuration for the surrogate model determines the flexibility of the function space . A function space with limited flexibility may not include a good approximation to the true model (⋅).\\n(2) by Monte Carlo simulation with the surrogate model.\\n5. Check Convergence . If a specified stopping condition is satisfied, terminate the algorithm.\\n6. Enrich the DoE . Add new training points into the DoE and proceed to Step 2.\\n2.2. Surrogate modeling techniques\\nSurrogate modeling represents the core of the framework. We ex-\\nThis capability stems efficient coefficient estimation techniques, such as least-square regression, or least angle regression [75]. In contrast, GP requires hyper-parameter tuning for the covariance kernel, limiting its scalability to higher dimensions.\\nA comparison of surrogate modeling accuracy for distribution estimation using different uniform designs. The error measure 𝜖𝐹 is described by Eq. (5).\\nand the second sets the maximum DoE size to min{100 + 𝑁 × 20 , 300}. The first criterion entails hyper-parameters such as thresholds and the number of consecutive triggers for the stopping criterion.\\nHence, we employ only the second criterion to stop the algorithm, facilitating the collection of data to analyze the optimal hyper-parameter settings for the first stop criterion. Additionally, we have increased the maximum model evaluations for Examples #3, #5, and #6 to 300 to accommo- date their slower convergence rates.\\nNext, the surrogates are compared across the 64 cases regarding\\nthe efficiency metric. The surrogate using the least number of model evaluations to converge (i.e., 𝜖𝐹 ≤ 𝜖 tol 𝐹 ) is labeled as the winner. Then,\\nwe compute the proportion of cases in which the given surrogate wins the comparisons. The results are presented in Fig. 5.\\nTable 1 The average number of model evaluations required to converge ( 𝜖𝐹 ≤ 𝜖 tol 𝐹 = 0 . 1) across 12 different solutions (combining 3 surrogate models and 4 DoE enrichment methods) over 16 benchmark examples. The symbol ‘‘–’’ represents that the solution fails to converge.\\nGP PCE PCK eg MoV t-LF g-LF MD MoV t-LF g-LF MD MoV t-LF g-LF MD\\nThe average number of model evaluations required to converge ( 𝜖𝐹 ≤ 𝜖 tol 𝐹 = 0 . 1 ) when using the enrichment method MD. The bar is not shown if the method fails to converge within the given maximum number of iterations .\\nFig. 7. Comparison of DoE enrichment methods in terms of accuracy.\\nThe efficiency metric, similar to that in the previous two subsections, is evaluated on each run. The cost metric is defined as the mean value of the normalized number of model evaluations. The normalized results are obtained by dividing the number of model evaluations by 𝑁 min  (i.e., the minimum number of model evaluations\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10123951\",\n      \"title\": \"Enhancing Random Search with Surrogate Models for ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Enhancing Random Search with Surrogate Models for Lipschitz\\nContinuous Optimization\\nQi Zhang and Jiaqiao Hu, Member, IEEE\\nAbstract — We propose a random search algorithm \\nCONFIDENTIAL. Limited circulation. For review only.\\nManuscript 110 submitted to 2019 IEEE 15th International Conference on Automation\\nScience and Engineering (CASE). Rece\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/2632-2153/ae2e7b/pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"Uncertainty quantification of surrogate models using conformal prediction\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2026-01-30\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Verification and validation for trustworthy scientific machine learning John D Jakeman, Lorena A Barba, Joaquim R R A Martins et al.\\nAbstract Data-driven surrogate models offer fast, inexpensive approximations to complex numerical and experimental systems but typically lack uncertainty quantification, limiting their reliabil- ity in\\nVerification and validation for trustworthy scientific machine learning John D Jakeman, Lorena A Barba, Joaquim R R A Martins et al.\\n20 million dimensions).\\nVerification and validation for trustworthy scientific machine learning John D Jakeman, Lorena A Barba, Joaquim R R A Martins et al.\\nThrough experiments of increasing complexity, we show guar- anteed marginal coverage irrespective of model choice (deterministic or probabilistic), output dimen- sionality (up to 20 million dimensions),\\nVerification and validation for trustworthy scientific machine learning John D Jakeman, Lorena A Barba, Joaquim R R A Martins et al.\\n∗ indicates out-of-distribution evaluation where calibration and training distributions differ. PT: pre-trained model; FT: fine-tuned model.\\nTable 1 presents our main empirical findings.\\nVerification and validation for trustworthy scientific machine learning John D Jakeman, Lorena A Barba, Joaquim R R A Martins et al.\\nA limitation of scalar uncertainty estimates is that there are no samples of the distribution over the atmospheric state.\\nVerification and validation for trustworthy scientific machine learning John D Jakeman, Lorena A Barba, Joaquim R R A Martins et al.\\nThis validity is particularly crucial when surrogate models transition from research to production environments where retraining opportunities are limited.\\nModel-agnostic and scalable.\\nVerification and validation for trustworthy scientific machine learning John D Jakeman, Lorena A Barba, Joaquim R R A Martins et al.\\nInput — (50, 20, 200) Encoder 1 Conv1d/BatchNorm1d/Tanh (50, 32, 200) Pool 1 MaxPool1d (50, 32, 200) Encoder 2 Conv1d/BatchNorm1d/Tanh (50, 64, 100) Pool 2 MaxPool1d (50, 64, 100) Encoder 3\\nVerification and validation for trustworthy scientific machine learning John D Jakeman, Lorena A Barba, Joaquim R R A Martins et al.\\nConv1d/BatchNorm1d/Tanh (50, 128, 50) Pool 3 MaxPool1d (50, 128, 50) Encoder 4 Conv1d/BatchNorm1d/Tanh (50, 256, 25) Pool 4 MaxPool1d (50, 256, 25 Bottleneck Conv1d/BatchNorm1d/Tanh (50, 512, 12) Decoder 4\\nVerification and validation for trustworthy scientific machine learning John D Jakeman, Lorena A Barba, Joaquim R R A Martins et al.\\nFor this experi- ment, we calibrate the limited area weather model using predictions for September 2021, and evaluate on January 2023.\\nVerification and validation for trustworthy scientific machine learning John D Jakeman, Lorena A Barba, Joaquim R R A Martins et al.\\nMach. Learn.: Sci. Technol. 7 (2026) 015025 V Gopakumar et al\\nFigure 33. Empirical coverage for limited area weather forecasting models, when calibrated on September and evaluating cover- age on January.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12124525\",\n      \"title\": \"Optimization of non-smooth functions via differentiable surrogates\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2025-05-30\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"This characteristic makes traditional gradient-based optimization methods inapplicable, necessitating the use of derivative-free optimization (DFO) techniques or heuristic algorithms. Although these approaches can eventually find satisfactory solutions, they generally require numerous function evaluations and provide limited convergence guarantees.\\n[28] introduced a deep learning surrogate model for stochastic simulations. Their approach uses a generative network combined with a conditional maximum mean discrepancy loss function to model stochastic outputs without assumptions about probability distributions.\\nThe selection of appropriate surrogate modeling techniques has traditionally depended on domain expertise [31]. While many comparative studies exist, most evaluate only specific models on limited applications [32].\\nWe select m values of either 5 or 10, and n values of either 10 or 15. For the equality constraints, we have:\\nWe follow the procedure described in Sect 3. First consider our data generation process: we created a synthetic dataset of 50,000 data points using a truncated normal distribution within the range [0,10].\\nAlthough offset by faster optimization runs, the process is computationally intensive for high-dimensional inputs, potentially limiting real-time or time-constrained applications. Moreover, performance may be sensitive to the neural network architecture and training parameters, which were not exhaustively explored in this study.\\nThe convergence of variable metric methods for nonlinearly constrained optimization calculations. Nonlinear Program. 1978;3:27–63. doi:. DOI: 10.1016/b978-0-12-468660-1.50007-4\\n50. Meunier L, Rakotoarison H, Wong PK, Roziere B, Rapin J, Teytaud O, et al.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2404.00053\",\n      \"title\": \"Adaptive Computing for Scale-up Problems\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Olga Doronina is a Data Scientist at NREL at Golden, CO, 80401, USA. Her research interests in- clude scientific machine learning, surrogate modeling and sensitivity analysis.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://par.nsf.gov/biblio/10187857-surrogate-model-selection-design-space-approximation-surrogate-based-optimization\",\n      \"title\": \"Surrogate Model Selection for Design Space Approximation and Surrogate-based Optimization | NSF Public Access Repository\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2019-07-01\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Surrogate models are used to map input data to output data when the actual relationship between the two is unknown or computationally expensive to evaluate for sensitivity analysis, uncertainty propagation and surrogate based optimization.\\nThis work evaluates the performance of eight surrogate modeling techniques for design space approximation and surrogate based optimization applications over a set of generated datasets with known characteristics.\\nFast inference of numerical model parameters from data is an important prerequisite to generate predictive models for a wide range of applications. Use of sampling-based approaches such as Markov chain Monte Carlo may become intractable when each likelihood evaluation is computationally expensive.\\nTo reduce the computational cost without sacrificing inferential accuracy, we propose Normalizing Flow with Adaptive Surrogate (NoFAS), an optimization strategy that alternatively updates the normalizing flow parameters and surrogate model parameters.\\nTraditionally, surrogate models require that all training data conform to the same parametrization (e.g., design variables), limiting design freedom and prohibiting the reuse of historical data. In response, this article proposes graph-based surrogate models (GSMs) for trusses.\\nThis work evaluates the performance of eight surrogate modeling techniques for design space approximation and surrogate based optimization applications over a set of generated datasets with known characteristics.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.1145/3512290.3528814\",\n      \"title\": \"Guiding surrogate-assisted multi-objective optimisation with ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Guiding surrogate-assisted multi-objective optimisation with ...\\nJul 8, 2022 · This drives an exploitative multi-surrogate model to quickly converge to solutions favourable to the decision maker, without relying on\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://purl.stanford.edu/bb876gs9072\",\n      \"title\": \"Deep neural network surrogates for inverse problems | Stanford Digital Repository\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Summary Inverse problems in subsurface flow are generally challenging due to the need for a large number of expensive numerical solutions to partial differential equations (PDEs). Inverse modeling typically consists of generating realizations of the unknown model parameters and matching the corresponding model's prediction to the measurements.\\nThe latter can be ameliorated either by devising inversion frameworks that require fewer forward model runs to converge, or by constructing a much more efficient forward surrogate model that replaces the PDE solver.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2108.00002\",\n      \"title\": \"Bayesian Optimization in Materials Science: A Survey\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Bayesian Optimization in Materials Science: A Survey testing a new material and require specialized equipment and skilled operators. At the heart of modern Bayesian optimization approaches are machine- learning-induced surrogate models, that learn to emulate the black-box process to be optimized based on a small number of evaluations. Such surrogate models have a long tradition in materials science where they helped in the develop- ment of new materials and designs long before the advent of machine learning. In modern Bayesian optimization, a surrogate model is fit based on the results of evaluating the black-box process at different points in the hyperparameter space. Such an initial design could be random, or based on another design of experi- ments approach. In most cases, this surrogate model is induced by a machine learning algorithm; Gaussian Processes and random forests are popular choices. 2 The expensive black-box function is then evaluated at the point proposed in this way to obtain ground-truth data that is combined with the data from the initial design. The updated data is used to obtain the surrogate model for the next iteration of the optimization process. The incremental augmentation of the data the surrogate model takes into account iteratively improves and refines it for the areas of interest, yielding more accurate approximations of the black-box function and better optimization results. The Bayesian optimization process can be stopped at any time when the desired quality is achieved or resources are exhausted.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_ea962aa48ffe7fg2I6fW6DcPEb\"\n}"
